ess: Mathematical Sciences Research Institute, 17 Gauss Way, Berkeley CA 94720, United States E-mail address: vschlue@math.utoronto.ca"]}}],"number_of_pages":47,"legacy_creation_date":"2012-07-25","preprint_date":"2012-07","author_count":1,"public_notes":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"Substantially extended results for Kerr de Sitter backgrounds, and\n more general expanding cosmologies without symmetries (Section 5);\n applications to Klein-Gordon equations added (Section 7); Introduction\n (Section 2.3.2) and references updated; 45 pages, 7 figures"}],"earliest_date":"2012-07","refereed":true,"external_system_identifiers":[{"schema":"MSNET","value":"3306609"},{"schema":"SPIRES","value":"SPIRES-9674314"}],"facet_author_name":["1904815_Volker Schlue"],"core":true,"license":[{"imposing":"arXiv","url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/"},{"license":"arXiv nonexclusive-distrib 1.0","material":"preprint","url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/"}],"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:1123789","updated":"2024-10-01T16:31:02.636803"},"journal_title_variants":["Commun.Math.Phys.","Commun. Math. Phys."],"arxiv_eprints":[{"categories":["gr-qc","math.AP","math.DG"],"value":"1207.6055"}],"referenced_authors_bais":["M.Dafermos.1","S.Dyatlov.1","Stephen.W.Hawking.1","K.Lake.1","J.Luk.1","George.F.R.Ellis.1","C.Bunster.1","Helmut.Friedrich.1","J.Speck.3","R.C.Roeder.1","J.A.Valiente.Kroon.1","M.Henneaux.1","G.W.Gibbons.1","Demetrios.Christodoulou.1","Artur.Alho.1","S.Aretakis.1","I.Rodnianski.1","F.C.Mena.1","C.Lubbe.2","A.Vasy.1"],"figures":[{"filename":"cauchy_expanding.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Global geometry of Schwarzschild de Sitter. The expanding region is the domain bounded in the past by the cosmological horizons $\\mathcal{C}^+$ and $\\bar{\\mathcal{C}^+}$ and to the future by the spacelike hypersurface $\\Sigma^+$ at infinity. $\\mathcal{H}^+$ and $\\bar{\\mathcal{H}}^+$ denote the event horizons of the adjacent black hole interiors.","label":"fig:cauchyexpanding","source":"arxiv","key":"93a3caa584a4c03b59bcc6532a4f4204","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/93a3caa584a4c03b59bcc6532a4f4204"},{"filename":"rs_dn.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Causal geometry of the cosmological region $\\mathcal{R}=\\{0<uv<1\\}$ and the adjacent static exteriors $\\mathcal{S}=\\{uv<0\\}$. The expanding region $\\mathcal{R}^+$ is the component of $\\mathcal{R}$ to the future of the cosmological horizons $\\mathcal{C}=\\{v=0\\}$ and $\\bar{\\mathcal{C}}=\\{u=0\\}$, and the future boundary $\\Sigma^+$ of $\\mathcal{R}^+$ is represented by the hyperbola $uv=1$.","label":"fig:dn","source":"arxiv","key":"f5176efeaf04229a3b35b7376951fb53","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/f5176efeaf04229a3b35b7376951fb53"},{"filename":"not_redshift_props.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Notation for hypersurfaces in Propositions~\\ref{prop:r:expanding}-\\ref{prop:r:static}.","label":"fig:not:redshift:props","source":"arxiv","key":"173f92add1be8e2d580e809753887533","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/173f92add1be8e2d580e809753887533"},{"filename":"notation_segments.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Notation for segments of the cosmological horizon $\\mathcal{C}^+$ and the future boundary $\\Sigma^+$.","label":"fig:not:segments","source":"arxiv","key":"009480d1369793a473161e17dc8a3ad2","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/009480d1369793a473161e17dc8a3ad2"},{"filename":"localise_not.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Localisation of the global and local redshift effect.","label":"fig:localise","source":"arxiv","key":"d311a0fda304e7a5cf29c1d18b151f30","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/d311a0fda304e7a5cf29c1d18b151f30"},{"filename":"cauchy_expanding-G.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Global redshift argument on a general class of expanding spacetimes $\\mathcal{G}_a^{(\\delta)}$.","label":"fig:appl:R","source":"arxiv","key":"3ac947a25f64c447dbf109f610aa3dcf","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/3ac947a25f64c447dbf109f610aa3dcf"},{"filename":"penrosedesitter.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Penrose diagram of de Sitter depicted as a spherically symmetric spacetime with respect to a chosen timelike geodesic $\\Gamma$.","label":"figure:thm:ds","source":"arxiv","key":"c2718e3cad98df70c96182bcfb22f8f0","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/c2718e3cad98df70c96182bcfb22f8f0"}],"legacy_version":"20180607211133.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"Gravitation and Cosmology"},{"term":"Math and Math Physics"}],"first_author":{"full_name":"Schlue, Volker","last_name":"Schlue","first_name":"Volker","recid":1904815},"control_number":1123789,"dois":[{"source":"bibmatch","value":"10.1007/s00220-014-2154-2"},{"material":"publication","source":"arXiv","value":"10.1007/s00220-014-2154-2"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Schlue:2012pq"],"abstracts":[{"source":"Springer","value":"In this global study of solutions to the linear wave equation on Schwarzschild de Sitter spacetimes we attend to the cosmological region of spacetime which is bounded in the past by cosmological horizons and to the future by a spacelike hypersurface at infinity. We prove an energy estimate capturing the expansion of that region, which combined with earlier results for the static region, yields a global boundedness result for linear waves. It asserts that a general finite energy solution to the global initial value problem has a limit on the future boundary at infinity that can be viewed as a function on the standard cylinder with finite energy, and that, moreover, any decay along the cosmological horizon is inherited along the future boundary. In particular, we exhibit an explicit nonvanishing quantity on the future boundary of the spacetime consistent with our expectations for the nonlinear stability problem. Our results apply to a large class of expanding cosmologies near the Schwarzschild de Sitter geometry, in particular subextremal Kerr de Sitter spacetimes.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"Springer"}},{"source":"arXiv","value":"In this global study of solutions to the linear wave equation on Schwarzschild de Sitter spacetimes we attend to the cosmological region of spacetime which is bounded in the past by cosmological horizons and to the future by a spacelike hypersurface at infinity. We prove an energy estimate capturing the expansion of that region which combined with earlier results for the static region yields a global boundedness result for linear waves. It asserts that a general finite energy solution to the global initial value problem has a limit on the future boundary at infinity that can be viewed as a function on the standard cylinder with finite energy, and that moreover any decay along the cosmological horizon is inherited along the future boundary. In particular, we exhibit an explicit nonvanishing quantity on the future boundary of the spacetime consistent with our expectations for the nonlinear stability problem. Our results apply to a large class of expanding cosmologies near the Schwarzschild de Sitter geometry, in particular subextremal Kerr de Sitter spacetimes.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["gr-qc"],"titles":[{"title":"Global Results for Linear Waves on Expanding Kerr and Schwarzschild de Sitter Cosmologies"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Global results for linear waves on expanding Schwarzschild de Sitter cosmologies"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Global results for linear waves on expanding Kerr and Schwarzschild de Sitter cosmologies"}],"imprints":[{"date":"2014-08-19"}],"curated":true}},{"links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1637357?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1637357?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1637357?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1637357?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1637357?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1637357?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A1637357"},"created":"2017-11-22T00:00:00+00:00","id":"1637357","updated":"2023-03-07T04:13:12.640294+00:00","metadata":{"citation_count":67,"citation_count_without_self_citations":66,"authors":[{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"klainerman, sergiu","full_name":"Klainerman, Sergiu","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1050880"},"ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"S.Klainerman.1"}],"last_name":"Klainerman","signature_block":"CLANARNANs","uuid":"42229797-f1b6-4486-95d9-96b7f046aaff","first_name":"Sergiu","recid":1050880},{"full_name_unicode_normalized":"szeftel, jeremie","full_name":"Szeftel, Jeremie","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/2133496"},"ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"J.Szeftel.2"}],"last_name":"Szeftel","signature_block":"SAFTALj","uuid":"b1f4c12b-6959-40f4-82a1-c60b3fff29e4","first_name":"Jeremie","recid":2133496}],"citeable":true,"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","references":[{"reference":{"label":"1","misc":["private communication"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Aksteiner, S."}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","value":"[1] S. Aksteiner, private communication."}]},{"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"1611.08291","label":"2","misc":["Gaugeinvariant perturbations of Schwarzschild spacetime, arXiv"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Aksteiner, S."},{"full_name":"Andersson, L."},{"full_name":"Bäckdahl, T."},{"full_name":"Shah, A.G."},{"full_name":"Whiting, B.F."}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","value":"[2] S. Aksteiner, L. Andersson, T. B¨ ackdahl, A. G. Shah and B. F. Whiting, Gaugeinvariant perturbations of Schwarzschild spacetime, arXiv.1611.08291."}],"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1500179"}},{"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"0908.2265","label":"3","misc":["Hidden symmetries and decay for the wave equation on the Kerr spacetime / to appear in A