el":"23","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"201","page_end":"655","year":1999,"page_start":"619","journal_title":"Commun.Math.Phys."},"misc":["The initial boundary value problem for Einstein’s vacuum field equation"],"authors":[{"full_name":"H, Friedrich"},{"full_name":"G., Nagy"}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","value":"[23] Friedrich H, Nagy G. 1999 The initial boundary value problem for Einstein’s vacuum field equation. Commun. Math. Phys. 201, 619-655, DOI: 10.1007/s002200050571 , ,"}],"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/483566"}},{"reference":{"imprint":{"publisher":"Springer"},"label":"24","publication_info":{"year":1989},"misc":["Lawden DF.Elliptic functions and applications. Berlin, Germany:"]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","value":"[24] Lawden DF. 1989 Elliptic functions and applications. Berlin, Germany: Springer. ,"}]},{"reference":{"label":"25","publication_info":{"year":2014},"misc":["Martín-García JM.See www.xact.es"]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","value":"[25] Martín-García JM. 2014 See www.xact.es."}]},{"reference":{"dois":["10.1007/s10714-020-02734-7"],"label":"26","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"52","artid":"85","year":2020,"page_start":"85","journal_title":"Gen.Rel.Grav."},"misc":["Valiente Kroon JA, Zhao P","Improved existence for the characteristic initial value problem with the conformal Einstein field equations"],"authors":[{"full_name":"D, Hilditch"}]},"raw_refs":[{"schema":"text","value":"[26] Hilditch D, Valiente Kroon JA, Zhao P. 2020 Improved existence for the characteristic initial value problem with the conformal Einstein field equations. Gen. Rel. Grav. 52, 85, DOI: 10.1007/s10714-020-02734-7 , ,"}],"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1802751"}}],"number_of_pages":23,"preprint_date":"2023-08-09","author_count":1,"public_notes":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"23 pages, 4 figures. Submitted to Philosophical Transactions A issue:\n \"At the interface of asymptotics, conformal methods and analysis in general\n relativity\". arXiv admin note: substantial text overlap with arXiv:2302.04004"}],"earliest_date":"2023-08-09","refereed":true,"facet_author_name":["1920205_Marica Minucci"],"license":[{"license":"CC-BY-4.0","imposing":"Royal Society","url":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"},{"license":"CC BY 4.0","material":"preprint","url":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}],"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:2686641","updated":"2026-02-09T13:18:26.118719"},"journal_title_variants":["Phil. Trans. Roy. Soc. Lond. A","Phil.Trans.Roy.Soc.Lond.A"],"arxiv_eprints":[{"categories":["gr-qc","math-ph","math.MP"],"value":"2308.04692"}],"referenced_authors_bais":["G.Nagy.1","Marica.Minucci.1","F.C.Mena.1","J.Joudioux.1","C.Lubbe.2","A.Garicia.Parrado.Gomez.Lobo.1","J.A.Valiente.Kroon.1","M.Thaller.1","Helmut.Friedrich.1","P.Zhao.17","E.Gasperin.1","D.Hilditch.1","Artur.Alho.1"],"inspire_categories":[{"term":"Gravitation and Cosmology","source":"arxiv"},{"term":"Math and Math Physics","source":"arxiv"}],"figures":[{"filename":"PenroseDiagram.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Penrose diagram of the background solution. The conformal representation discussed in the main text has compact sections of negative scalar curvature. The vertical lines $\\Gamma_1$ and $\\Gamma_2$ correspond to axes of symmetry. The solution has a singularity in the past and a spacelike future conformal boundary. Hence, in our discussion we only consider future evolution of the initial hypersurface $\\mathcal{S}_\\star$.","source":"arxiv","label":"Figure:PenroseDiagram","key":"998aa263f580eebde7e14c3b5e0aa583","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/998aa263f580eebde7e14c3b5e0aa583"},{"filename":"SubextremalSchwarzschildDeSitter.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Penrose diagram of the sub-extremal Schwarzschild-de Sitter spacetime. The serrated line denotes the location of the singularity; the continuous black line denotes the conformal boundary; the dashed line shows the location of the black hole and Cosmological horizons denoted by $\\mathcal{H}_{b}$ and $\\mathcal{H}_{c}$ respectively. As described in the main text, these horizons are located at $r=r_{b}$ and $r=r_{c}$. The excluded points $\\mathcal{Q}$ and $\\mathcal{Q'}$ where the singularity seems to meet the conformal boundary correspond to asymptotic regions of the spacetime that does not belong to the singularity nor the conformal boundary.","source":"arxiv","label":"Figure:SdSPenroseDiagram","key":"f425fc730ccc58cc585fe2a49a4c4477","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/f425fc730ccc58cc585fe2a49a4c4477"},{"filename":"PenroseDiagramOfTheConformalGeodesicsOfsSdS.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The conformal geodesics are plotted on the Penrose diagram of the Cosmological region of the sub-extremal Schwarzschild-de Sitter spacetime. The purple line represents the initial hypersurface $\\mathcal{S}_\\star$ corresponding to $r=r_\\star$. The red lines represent conformal geodesics with constant time leaving this initial hypersurface.","source":"arxiv","label":"Figure:PenroseDiagramConformalGeodesics","key":"55633821bd2accf79ca505b359d0fe17","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/55633821bd2accf79ca505b359d0fe17"},{"filename":"FutureDomainOfDependenceWithExtension.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The red curves identify the timelike hypersurfaces $\\mathcal{T}_{-2t_\\bullet}$ and $\\mathcal{T}_{2t_\\bullet}$. The resulting spacetime manifold $\\bar{M}_\\bullet$ has compact spatial sections, $\\bar{\\mathcal{S}}_z$,with the topology of $\\mathbb{S}^1\\times\\mathbb{S}^2$.","source":"arxiv","label":"Figure:FutureDomainofDependenceWithExtension","key":"82a0add44d8b0950ecc42099a1a9360f","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/82a0add44d8b0950ecc42099a1a9360f"}],"first_author":{"emails":["m.minucci@qmul.ac.uk"],"full_name":"Minucci, Marica","ids":[{"schema":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7095-5115"},{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"Marica.Minucci.1"}],"last_name":"Minucci","first_name":"Marica","recid":1920205},"control_number":2686641,"dois":[{"source":"Royal Society","value":"10.1098/rsta.2023.0040"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Minucci:2023pih"],"abstracts":[{"source":"Royal Society","value":"This article provides a discussion on the construction of conformal Gaussian gauge systems to study the evolution of solutions to the Einstein field equations with positive Cosmological constant. This is done by means of a gauge based on the properties of conformal geodesics. The use of this gauge, combined with the extended conformal Einstein field equations, yields evolution equations in the form of a symmetric hyperbolic system for which standard Cauchy stability results can be employed. This strategy is used to study the global properties of de Sitter-like spacetimes with constant negative scalar curvature. It is then adapted to study the evolution of the Schwarzschild–de Sitter spacetime in the static region near the conformal boundary. This review is based on Minucci et al. 2021 Class. Quantum Grav. 38, 145026. (doi:10.1088/1361-6382/ac0356) and Minucci et al. 2023 Class. Quantum Grav. 40, 145005. (doi:10.1088/1361-6382/acdb3f).This article is part of a discussion meeting issue ‘At the interface of asymptotics, conformal methods and analysis in general relativity’.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"Royal Society"}},{"source":"arXiv","value":"This article provides a discussion on the construction of conformal Gaussian gauge systems to study the evolution of solutions to the Einstein field equations with positive Cosmological constant. This is done by means of a gauge based on the properties of conformal geodesics. The use of this gauge, combined with the extended conformal Einstein field equations, yields evolution equations in the form of a symmetric hyperbolic system for which standard Cauchy stability results can be employed. This strategy is used to study the global properties of de Sitter-like spacetimes with constant negative scalar curvature. It is then adapted to study the evolution of the Schwarzschild-de Sitter spacetime in the static region near the conformal boundary. This review is based on Class. Quantum Grav. 38 145026 and Class. Quantum Grav. 40 145005.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["gr-qc"],"titles":[{"source":"Royal Society","title":"Conformal geodesics and the evolution of spacetimes with positive Cosmological constant"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Conformal geodesics and the evolution of spacetimes with positive Cosmological constant"}],"imprints":[{"date":"2024-03-04"}],"curated":false}},{"links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3194756?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3194756?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3194756?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3194756?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3194756?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/3194756?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A3194756"},"created":"2026-08-24T08:23:35.513176+00:00","id":"3194756","updated":"2026-08-24T08:23:35.713878+00:00","metadata":{"citation_count":0,"publication_info":[{"journal_volume":"43","artid":"165010","year":2026,"journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1214779"},"journal_issue":"16","journal_title":"Class.Quant.Grav."}],"citation_count_without_self_citations":0,"authors":[{"raw_affiliations":[{"value":", Committed Mathematics Team, Research Unit in Mathematics and Applications Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, , University of Dschang, , P O Box 67 Dschang, , Cameroon"},{"value":", Department of Mathematics, Higher Teacher Training College, , University of Bertoua, , P O Box 652 Bertoua, , Cameroon"}],"full_name_unicode_normalized":"djiodjo-seugmo, guichard","full_name":"Djiodjo-Seugmo, Gui